<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vietnam War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> War was fought from the mid-1950s to 1975 between communist North Vietnam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> on one side and South Vietnam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on the other, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and others sending forces. American combat troops fought from 1965 until their withdrawal in 1973, and the war ended with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 and Vietnam's reunification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vietnam-war.jpg" alt="Vietnam War" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Air Force (Operation Holly 1970 (Folder 13 of 15), sheet 182) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Army_UH-1H_Hueys_insert_ARVN_troops_at_Kh%C3%A2m_%C4%90%E1%BB%A9c,_Vietnam,_12_July_1970_(79431435).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> had been under French control as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> since the 1880s. After the French defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, Vietnam's independence was affirmed at the 1954 Geneva Conference, which divided the country at the 17th parallel. The communists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> took power in North Vietnam, while an anticommunist regime under Ngô Đình Diệm took control of South Vietnam with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> backing. The North Vietnamese supplied and eventually directed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>, a communist front in the south that intensified a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war from 1957. The war was at once a postcolonial war of national liberation, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and a civil war. In 1963 Diệm was killed in a US-backed military coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After the Gulf of Tonkin incident of August 1964, the US Congress passed a resolution that gave President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> authority to increase the military presence without declaring war. On 8 March 1965, 3,500 US Marines landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Da Nang</a>, the start of the ground war, and deployment rose to 536,000 by 1969. The Tet Offensive of 1968 was a tactical defeat for the communists but contributed to growing American opposition to the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> began Vietnamization from 1969, in which an expanded South Vietnamese army took over the fighting as US forces withdrew. Fighting spilled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> of 1973 the last American forces left, but fighting continued. In March 1975 North Vietnamese forces opened an offensive in the Central Highlands, and the South Vietnamese army collapsed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> fell on 30 April 1975, after the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> evacuation in history, Operation Frequent Wind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>North and South Vietnam were merged in July 1976 as the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. Estimates of the dead vary widely. About 58,220 US service members died, and estimates run from 275,000 to 310,000 Cambodians and 20,000 to 62,000 Laotians. The war was marked by atrocities, among them the massacres at Huế and Mỹ Lai, and some 20 percent of South Vietnam's jungle was sprayed with toxic herbicides, which led to significant health problems. Political repression and flawed economic policies after the war precipitated the flight of the Vietnamese boat people, of whom about 250,000 perished at sea. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the war gave rise to Vietnam syndrome, an aversion to overseas military involvement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vietnam War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Zealand forces fought in the Malayan Emergency and in the Indonesian–Malaysian confrontation of the 1960s, and from 1964 engineers, artillery and later infantry served in the <b>Vietnam War</b>, a commitment that provoked a large protest movement; the last troops were withdrawn by the end of 1972.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Socialist Republic of Vietnam was born from the victory of the north in the <b>Vietnam War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>King also spoke out against the <b>Vietnam War</b> in 1967 and planned a Poor People’s Campaign, while the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover, which had wiretapped him since 1963, sought to discredit him.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1968 the <b>Vietnam War</b> had given rise to the New Left, critical of both the Soviet Union and social democracy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Judy Collins recorded it in 1970 in opposition to the <b>Vietnam War</b>, and it reached number 15 on the Billboard Hot 100; an instrumental by the Royal Scots Dragoon Guards spent five weeks at number one in the UK in 1972.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>Because of these images of peace, the spiritual has also been sung as an anti-war protest song, especially during the <b>Vietnam War</b>, and it appears in collections of socialist and labour songs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/down-by-the-riverside">Down by the Riverside</a></li></ul></section>
